{"task_id": "HumanEval/132", "solution": "```python\ndef is_nested(string):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the input string contains at least one valid nested bracket subsequence.\n    A valid pair `[]` is nested if it's contained within another currently open `[` bracket.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(string, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a string.\")\n\n    # Validate input characters for square brackets only.\n    for char in string:\n        if char not in ['[', ']']:\n            raise ValueError(\"Input string must contain only '[' and ']' characters.\")\n\n    # A string needs at least 4 characters to contain a nested pair like '[[]]'.\n    if len(string) < 4:\n        return False\n\n    stack = []\n    for char in string:\n        if char == '[':\n            stack.append(char)\n        elif char == ']':\n            if stack:\n                stack.pop()\n                # If the stack is not empty after popping, it means the current '[]' pair\n                # was enclosed within another open bracket, indicating nesting.\n                if stack:\n                    return True\n            else:\n                # An unmatched closing bracket invalidates the current potential sequence.\n                # Resetting the stack allows finding new nested pairs later in the string.\n                stack = []\n\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/135", "solution": "```python\ndef can_arrange(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the largest index i where arr[i] is strictly less than arr[i-1].\n    Returns -1 if the array is sorted in non-decreasing order or has fewer than two elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    if n < 2:\n        return -1\n\n    for i in range(n - 1, 0, -1):\n        if arr[i] < arr[i - 1]:\n            return i\n    return -1\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/136", "solution": "def largest_smallest_integers(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the largest negative integer and the smallest positive integer in a list.\n    Returns (largest_negative, smallest_positive), or None for either if not present.\n    \"\"\"\n    largest_neg = None\n    smallest_pos = None\n\n    negative_numbers = [x for x in lst if x < 0]\n    positive_numbers = [x for x in lst if x > 0]\n\n    if negative_numbers:\n        largest_neg = max(negative_numbers)\n\n    if positive_numbers:\n        smallest_pos = min(positive_numbers)\n\n    return (largest_neg, smallest_pos)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/136", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_smallest_integers(lst: list[int]) -> tuple[int | None, int | None]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the largest negative and smallest positive integers in a list.\n    Returns (largest_negative, smallest_positive), or None for missing types.\n    \"\"\"\n    largest_neg = None\n    smallest_pos = None\n\n    for num in lst:\n        if num < 0:\n            if largest_neg is None or num > largest_neg:\n                largest_neg = num\n        elif num > 0:\n            if smallest_pos is None or num < smallest_pos:\n                smallest_pos = num\n    return (largest_neg, smallest_pos)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/138", "solution": "```python\ndef is_equal_to_sum_even(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Evaluates whether the given number n can be written as the sum of exactly 4 positive even numbers.\n    Raises ValueError if n is not an integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n\n    # A number n can be the sum of 4 positive even numbers if it is even and at least 8.\n    # The smallest sum (2+2+2+2) is 8, and any sum of even numbers must be even.\n    return n >= 8 and n % 2 == 0\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/137", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import Union\n\ndef compare_one(a: Union[int, float, str], b: Union[int, float, str]) -> Union[int, float, str, None]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Compares two numerical values (int, float, or string) and returns the larger one,\n    preserving its original type. Returns None if the values are numerically equal.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _parse_number(value: Union[int, float, str]) -> float:\n        \"\"\"Helper to convert various types to float, handling string decimal separators.\"\"\"\n        if isinstance(value, (int, float)):\n            return float(value)\n        elif isinstance(value, str):\n            normalized_value = value.replace(',', '.')\n            try:\n                return float(normalized_value)\n            except ValueError as e:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Cannot convert string '{value}' to float.\") from e\n        else:\n            raise TypeError(f\"Unsupported type for comparison: {type(value)}\")\n\n    parsed_a = _parse_number(a)\n    parsed_b = _parse_number(b)\n\n    if parsed_a > parsed_b:\n        return a\n    elif parsed_b > parsed_a:\n        return b\n    else:\n        return None\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/122", "solution": "```python\ndef add_elements(arr, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a non-empty array of integers arr and an integer k, return\n    the sum of the elements with at most two digits from the first k elements of arr.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0\n    # Iterate through the first k elements as specified by the design.\n    for i in range(k):\n        element = arr[i]\n        # Check if the element's absolute value is at most 99, as per business rules.\n        if -99 <= element <= 99:\n            total_sum += element\n    return total_sum\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/138", "solution": "```python\ndef is_equal_to_sum_even(n):\n    \"\"\"Evaluate whether the given number n can be written as the sum of exactly 4 positive even numbers.\n    A positive even number is an even integer greater than 0 (e.g., 2, 4, 6...).\"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n    \n    # The smallest sum of 4 positive even numbers is 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 8.\n    if n < 8:\n        return False\n    \n    # The sum of any number of even numbers must be an even number.\n    if n % 2 != 0:\n        return False\n        \n    # If n is even and n >= 8, it can always be written as the sum of 4 positive even numbers.\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/137", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import Union\n\ndef compare_one(a: Union[int, float, str], b: Union[int, float, str]) -> Union[int, float, str, None]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Compares two numerical values and returns the larger in its original type.\n    Returns None if the values are numerically equal, handling comma/period decimal separators.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def _parse_value_to_float(value: Union[int, float, str]) -> float:\n        \"\"\"Converts a value to a float, processing string representations with optional comma decimal separators.\"\"\"\n        if isinstance(value, str):\n            normalized_value = value.replace(',', '.')\n            return float(normalized_value)\n        return float(value)\n\n    a_float = _parse_value_to_float(a)\n    b_float = _parse_value_to_float(b)\n\n    if a_float > b_float:\n        return a\n    elif b_float > a_float:\n        return b\n    else:\n        return None\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/139", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef special_factorial(n):\n    \"\"\"The Brazilian factorial is defined as: `n! * (n-1)! * (n-2)! * ... * 1!`, where `n > 0`.\n    For example, `special_factorial(4)` returns 288.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int):\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be an integer.\")\n    if n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer (n > 0).\")\n\n    result = 1\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        result *= math.factorial(i)\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/139", "solution": "```python\nclass InvalidInputError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid input to special factorial calculation.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str):\n        super().__init__(message)\n\ndef special_factorial(n):\n    \"\"\"Computes the special factorial: n! * (n-1)! * ... * 1!. Input n must be a positive integer.\"\"\"\n\n    # Input validation based on FR1, FR3, and BR1\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 0:\n        raise InvalidInputError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    # Helper function mimicking FactorialCalculator.calculate logic\n    def _calculate_factorial(k: int) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Calculates the standard factorial k!. Input k must be a non-negative integer.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(k, int) or k < 0:\n            raise ValueError(\"Factorial input must be a non-negative integer.\")\n        \n        if k == 0:\n            return 1\n        \n        result = 1\n        for i in range(1, k + 1):\n            result *= i\n        return result\n\n    special_fact_result = 1\n    # Accumulate the product of factorials from 1! up to n!\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        current_factorial = _calculate_factorial(i)\n        special_fact_result *= current_factorial\n        \n    return special_fact_result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/140", "solution": "```python\nimport re\n\ndef fix_spaces(text):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a string text, replace all spaces in it with underscores, \n    and if a string has more than 2 consecutive spaces, \n    then replace all consecutive spaces with - \n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(text, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a string.\")\n\n    # Replace 3 or more consecutive spaces with a single hyphen.\n    result = re.sub(r' {3,}', '-', text)\n\n    # Replace exactly 2 consecutive spaces with two underscores.\n    result = re.sub(r' {2}', '__', result)\n\n    # Replace single spaces with underscores.\n    result = re.sub(r' ', '_', result)\n\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/141", "solution": "```python\ndef file_name_check(file_name):\n    \"\"\"Create a function which takes a string representing a file's name, and returns\n    'Yes' if the the file's name is valid, and returns 'No' otherwise.\n    A file's name is considered to be valid if and only if all the following conditions \n    are met:\n    - There should not be more than three digits ('0'-'9') in the file's name.\n    - The file's name contains exactly one dot '.'\n    - The substring before the dot should not be empty, and it starts with a letter from \n    the latin alphapet ('a'-'z' and 'A'-'Z').\n    - The substring after the dot should be one of these: ['txt', 'exe', 'dll']\n    Examples:\n    file_name_check(\"example.txt\") # => 'Yes'\n    file_name_check(\"1example.dll\") # => 'No' (the name should start with a latin alphapet letter)\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # FR1: Check if the file name contains no more than three digits.\n    digit_count = sum(1 for char in file_name if char.isdigit())\n    if digit_count > 3:\n        return 'No'\n\n    # FR2: Check if the file name contains exactly one dot.\n    if file_name.count('.') != 1:\n        return 'No'\n\n    # Split the file name into prefix and extension parts.\n    parts = file_name.split('.')\n    prefix = parts[0]\n    extension = parts[1]\n\n    # FR3: Check if the prefix is not empty and starts with a letter.\n    if not prefix or not prefix[0].isalpha():\n        return 'No'\n\n    # FR4: Check if the extension is one of the allowed types.\n    allowed_extensions = ['txt', 'exe', 'dll']\n    if extension not in allowed_extensions:\n        return 'No'\n\n    # FR5: All conditions met, return 'Yes'.\n    return 'Yes'\n```"}
