{"task_id": "HumanEval/148", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Optional, Tuple\n\nclass SolarSystem:\n    _planets: List[str] = [\n        \"Mercury\", \"Venus\", \"Earth\", \"Mars\", \"Jupiter\",\n        \"Saturn\", \"Uranus\", \"Neptune\"\n    ]\n    _planet_map: Dict[str, int] = {\n        planet: i for i, planet in enumerate(_planets)\n    }\n\n    def get_planet_index(self, planet_name: str) -> Optional[int]:\n        \"\"\"Returns the index of a planet, or None if not found.\"\"\"\n        return self._planet_map.get(planet_name)\n\n    def get_planets_between(self, index1: int, index2: int) -> Tuple[str, ...]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Returns a tuple of planets between the given indices (exclusive),\n        sorted by proximity to the Sun. Handles cases where index1 > index2.\n        \"\"\"\n        if index1 == index2:\n            return ()\n\n        start_index = min(index1, index2)\n        end_index = max(index1, index2)\n\n        # Exclusive of the start and end planets.\n        return tuple(self._planets[start_index + 1:end_index])\n\n# Create a singleton instance of SolarSystem\nsolar_system_instance = SolarSystem()\n\ndef bf(planet1: str, planet2: str) -> Tuple[str, ...]:\n    \"\"\"\n    There are eight planets in our solar system: the closerst to the Sun \n    is Mercury, the next one is Venus, then Earth, Mars, Jupiter, Saturn, \n    Uranus, Neptune.\n    Write a function that takes two planet names as strings planet1 and planet2. \n    The function should return a tuple containing all planets whose orbits are \n    located between the orbit of planet1 and the orbit of planet2, sorted by \n    the proximity to the sun. \n    The function should return an empty tuple if planet1 or planet2\n    are not correct planet names. \n    Examples\n    bf(\"Jupiter\", \"Neptune\") ==> (\"Saturn\", \"Uranus\")\n    bf(\"Earth\", \"Mercury\") ==> (\"Venus\")\n    bf(\"Mercury\", \"Uranus\") ==> (\"Venus\", \"Earth\", \"Mars\", \"Jupiter\", \"Saturn\")\n    \"\"\"\n    index1 = solar_system_instance.get_planet_index(planet1)\n    index2 = solar_system_instance.get_planet_index(planet2)\n\n    if index1 is None or index2 is None:\n        return ()\n\n    return solar_system_instance.get_planets_between(index1, index2)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/149", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import Any, List\n\nclass InvalidInputError(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid input to string processing functions.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, message: str):\n        super().__init__(message)\n        self.message = message\n\nclass InputValidator:\n    \"\"\"Provides utility methods for validating input data.\"\"\"\n    @staticmethod\n    def validate_string_list(lst: Any) -> None:\n        \"\"\"\n        Validates that the input is a list of strings.\n        Raises InvalidInputError if validation fails.\n        \"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(lst, list):\n            raise InvalidInputError(\"Input must be a list.\")\n        for item in lst:\n            if not isinstance(item, str):\n                raise InvalidInputError(\"All elements in the list must be strings.\")\n\nclass StringSorter:\n    \"\"\"Handles filtering and sorting operations on lists of strings.\"\"\"\n    @staticmethod\n    def filter_odd_length_strings(lst: List[str]) -> List[str]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Filters out strings that have odd lengths from the list.\n        Returns a new list containing only strings with even lengths.\n        \"\"\"\n        return [s for s in lst if len(s) % 2 == 0]\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def sort_strings(lst: List[str]) -> List[str]:\n        \"\"\"\n        Sorts the list of strings first by length then alphabetically.\n        Returns a new list containing the sorted strings.\n        \"\"\"\n        return sorted(lst, key=lambda s: (len(s), s))\n\ndef sorted_list_sum(lst):\n    \"\"\"Write a function that accepts a list of strings as a parameter,\n    deletes the strings that have odd lengths from it,\n    and returns the resulted list with a sorted order,\n    The list is always a list of strings and never an array of numbers,\n    and it may contain duplicates.\n    The order of the list should be ascending by length of each word, and you\n    should return the